oss six inpatient wards.\n\n## Methods\n\nWe conducted a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards. Wards introduced adaptive alerts according to local operational schedules; patients were not randomly assigned. Each intervention observation was matched to a usual-monitoring observation by ward and baseline risk.\n\nThe primary endpoint was retention of a usable overnight sensor record through 06:00. The analysis used complete cases and did not impute missing sensor records. Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory.\n\n## Results\n\nOvernight retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual monitoring, an adjusted absolute difference of 18.4 percentage points (95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7; p = 0.006) [D2].\n\nSensor dropout affected 8.7% of adaptive-alert observations and 3.1% of usual-monitoring observations. The primary analysis used complete cases without imputation, and the differential dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D3].\n\nWard-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory and were not preregistered; they do not establish effect heterogeneity [D3].\n\n## Discussion\n\nThis matched-cohort evaluation found that adaptive alerts were associated with higher overnight retention, but the nonrandomized design does not establish causation [D1][D2]. Differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward, so the observed difference should be interpreted cautiously [D3]. The ward-level subgroup analysis was exploratory and not preregistered, and it does not provide confirmatory evidence of effect heterogeneity [D3].\n\n## References\n\n[D1] Operations Consortium. Multisite rollout design record. 2026.\n\n[D2] Analysis Group. Revised overnight-retention model. 2026.\n\n[D3] Data Quality Team. Sensor-completeness audit. 2026.\n","expected_file_sha256":"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439","path":"paper.md"},"call":5,"candidate_id":"6e637d7454117bedbe1f8eb829da969cdd0edef12b9a54f79fb2e